Before July, BICoE already had some known source-level data duplication within individual quote payloads, for example duplicate driver or vehicle information within the same quote. These were understood as source-data quality issues and were different from the duplicate message patterns now under investigation.
From around 20 July, following the introduction of the MQS replay/recovery mechanism in Production, BICoE started to observe a different class of duplication at message-processing level.
At the same time, the incoming MQS header structure changed and no longer matched the format expected by the BICoE ingestion process. This interrupted normal ingestion. To avoid losing incoming data while the header issue was being corrected, BICoE temporarily diverted the Event Hub stream into a temporary EFST/landing table.
Once the header format was corrected, BICoE also introduced an ingestion deduplication control between the landing layer and the Bronze/DLT layer to prevent repeated messages from propagating downstream.
However, during the recovery activity, some data from the temporary landing area had already been reprocessed into the main processing path before the revised deduplication control was fully in place. This created a one-off historical duplicate population, represented by Scenario 4 in this document.
From the same July period onwards, BICoE also started observing several other replay-related duplicate patterns, including same-day replay, cross-day replay, exact duplicate messages and conflicting message variants such as the same MQSQuoteId + messageType with different quoteStep values.
The current BICoE ingestion deduplication is able to handle some of these patterns, particularly immediate/same-day and exact duplicate arrivals. However, other patterns can fall outside the current deduplication assumptions and propagate into downstream layers.
